Date: Fri, 14 May 93 14:43:47 PDT
From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: GAME: To play or not to play?

	 Perhaps; does anyone have experience of playing games for self-improvement?
	 What other games might one use for this purpose?

Role-playing games!  I designed characters that emphasized some
personality trait that I wanted to enhance, and then played them for a
while.  The activity really did enhance the traits that I wanted to
develop.  I got to experiment with those traits in a safe context
where I could exaggerate the trait in order to learn about it.

For instance, one was a dwarven fighter:  the property of dwarves that
I emphasized is that when they finally decide to do something, they
*will*not* stop.  This devlops a perserverance that is extremely
useful.

Another was a Seer character who never fought in battles ("I like to
watch" :-) because such engagement would distract him from the
perspective necessary to see surprises and long term goals.  He
quickly found more useful ways to aid the party even in pitched
battles because he was the only one keeping the goals in mind (how to
learn not to flame :-)

Characters where I chose a personality to emphasize were also the most
fun to play.  Because I had some defined personality traits, and a
reason to stick to them, I really *role* played and experienced events
from a different perspective than my own.  This emphasis makes the
game lots more fun, and incidentally made my characters more effective
because they knew what they were about.  Of course it drove my GM mad
when he would culminate an adventure in some really power magic item
that had lots of offensive powers or some such, and my Seer character
just wasn't interested in it.  The seer had gotten his reward by
seeing some amazing thing happen in the world, which he could then go
and relate to the university he worked at....

I recommend this activity to anyone who wants to strengthen aspects of
the personality.  It's also quite fun.

