Date:     Sat, 15 May 93 7:33:23 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Print Set-Up Time

At ISIL I run an offset printing press that Ben Franklin would have loved,
but it still has plenty of problems.  Harry's right that set-up time is
a major limiting factor.  When up and running, it can print up to 7,000
sheets of paper per hour.  However, changing color takes about half an hour,
for example, between cleaning it and getting the new color up and running.

Perry's also right that it would be too much trouble to have to make plates
for each and every printing - if only because it would cost so damn much
to dispose of them all!  ISIL published "A Liberty Primer," by Alan Burris
on our press, and perfect-bound them (which only means that we collated the
sheets, stacked them, and painted the left-hand side of the stack with glue,
slit the volumes apart with a knife, then slapped a cover on the outside of
'em).  We've also been perfect-binding the Freedom Network News (FNN) of late.

Consider: we recently did a run of 5,000 of our newsletters, which were 26
pages long - 13 sheets, both sides.  That's 65,000 pages, 130,000 runs through
the press.  We did this with 26 plates, one for each page.  Plates cost a
couple of bucks each.  Imagine if we had to make plates up specially for each
copy!  That'd run into about $260,000, with a low estimate of $2/plate!  That's
higher than ISIL's annual operating budget.  No way in hell could stores sell
250-page books for $5 or so and profit if they had 250 plates to dispose of
for each copy.

Dani Eder's idea is better than this, in that it would basically replace the
separate plates with a mechanical bitmap.  However, even assuming that the
resolution was good enough (with linotronic plates we can get about 1000 dpi
if we need to, although I think we settle for about 400 most of the time)
this still wouldn't suffice.

That would do the trick for one sheet at a time, maximum.  Assuming that it
only took such a machine one second to print both sides of a sheet, for only
a 250-sheet book, that's about a 5-minute printing job.  Then the pages have
to be collated, which wouldn't take too much longer, and then bound, which
still wouldn't add that much time.  But what about drying time for the ink
and the glue?

We could run the same sheet through, one side after the other, but the ink
on the first side won't be dry enough yet for it to run smoothly on the
second side.  We usually proceed on to the next sheet.  For example, with
the above newsletter, we did all the odd-numbered sides first, then went back
to the first sheet and did the even ones.  Without this drying time, the
sheets simply will get jammed up and shredded by the press.

Assuming the capacity to print both sides simultaneously, the sheet still
won't be ready to be bound together with others because the ink will offset
until dry enough.  Then once they're bound together, the binding glue won't
be dry for at least half an hour, if not hours later.

What's needed is something like cheap laserprinting and quick-drying binding
glue.  Sounds like the laserprinting ain't cheap enough, and I have no idea
if there is binding glue that dries fast enough.  Fast-drying glue will have
the additional difficulty, though, of possibly drying before getting used.
It would have to be sprayed on or something where it's stored in a liquid
state with no opportunity to dry out.

Through all this I've ignored problems with register, that is, the alignment
of the print, and the various demons which possess presses from time to time.
We need to call in an exorcist for ours, and soon!

I think we'll get to online books before we'll get instant hardcopy books.
CD-ROMS have the storage capacity, and monitors are starting to have the
resolution in order to serve this purpose.

One thing that could be integrated with this is to issue electronic books
that could be put through a voice synthesizer, so that you could listen to
your computer read them to you through your headphones or another speaker,
rather than having to look at a screen.  This would be better than current
audio books because you could search through the text visually until you
found the part you were interested in at a much higher speed.

